rns here.\n\nFirst, let's check if I can form any of the scoring patterns. For example, if I can complete a Pure Straight or something. But my hand is a mix of T's, B's, and W's. The melds are in T and W. The remaining tiles are W1, T4x2, B2, B3x2, B4, J2. Maybe I can form a Pure Straight in B's? B2, B3, B4 could form a Chi. If I have B2, B3x2, B4, then with another B3, I could form a Chi. But I have two B3s. So if I discard one B3, I can keep B2, B3, B4 for a possible Chi. Alternatively, maybe I can form a B2-B3-B4 Chi, which would be a chow. Then I have a Chi in T, a Peng in W, and a Chi in B. That would be three Chows, and then the remaining tiles would be W1 and J2. But W1 is a terminal, and J2 is a dragon. So that would be three Chows plus a pair. But I need four melds and a pair. Wait, three Chows plus a pair would be four melds? No, each Chi is a meld. So three Chows and a pair would be three melds and a pair. Wait, the winning hand requires four melds and a pair. So if I have three Chows (each a meld) and a pair, that's three melds and a pair. So I need one more meld. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form another meld. Let's see. The W1 is a single tile. J2 is a single tile. So if I discard a tile, maybe I can form a pair. Let's see: if I discard B3, then I have B2, B3, B4. That's a Chi. Then the melds would be Chi T3-4-5, Peng W2, Chi B2-3-4. Then the remaining tiles would be W1, J2. Those are two single tiles. So I need a pair. But I have W1 and J2 as singles. So unless I can form a pair from them, which I can't. So that's three melds and two singles. Not enough. So maybe I need to form another meld.\n\nWait, the W1 is a single. If I can form a pair with W1, but I only have one. So that's not possible. J2 is a single. So maybe the pair is in the J2? No, only one. So perhaps I need to form a fourth meld. Let's see. The remaining tiles after the three Chows would be W1 and J2. That's two tiles. So I need to form another meld. Maybe a Pung or Kong? But I do