nother B3 comes. But there are only 2 B3s left. If I play B3, maybe someone will take it, but if not, I can still form a Chow with B2 and B4. \n\nSame with B4. If I play B4, someone might take it to form a Chow. But since I have B3 and B2, discarding B4 could be risky. \n\nThe W1 and W6 are single tiles. W1 is a terminal tile. If I discard W1, it's a 1, which is a terminal. But there's 4 left. Maybe safer to discard W1 or W6, which are single tiles. \n\nJ2 is a Dragon tile. If I discard it, it might be taken by someone for a Pung if they have two J2s. But there are two J2s left. Since I have one, maybe it's safe to discard. \n\nSo, considering all, playing W1 or W6 might be safer since they are single and not part of any sequences. Alternatively, playing J2 if I think it's not needed. \n\nBut let's think about the points. My current hand has a Pung of W2s (which gives 2 points for Seat Wind if it's East, which it is). Also, the T3-4-5 is a Chow. The B2-3-4 could be a Chow if I can get another tile. If I can form another Chow or Pung, that would add points. \n\nIf I discard T4, I lose the pair, which is part of my hand. But maybe I can form another pair with another tile. \n\nWait, my current hand is 7 tiles. I need to play one to have 6, then draw to reach 14. So after playing, I'll draw a tile. If I play T4, then I have W1, W6, T4 (one), B2, B3, B4, J2. Then draw. If I draw another T4, I can form a Pung. But there are two T4s left. \n\nAlternatively, if I play B3 or B4, then maybe I can form a B2-3-4 Chow if I draw the right tile. \n\nBut maybe the safest play is to discard a tile that's not part of any sequences. W1 is a terminal tile, single, and if discarded, it's less likely to be needed for a Chow. Similarly, W6 is a single. \n\nBut I have to choose between the valid actions. The valid actions are Play B3; Play B4; Play B2; Play W6; Play W1; Play J2; Play T4. \n\nIf I play W1, it's a safe discard. If I play J2, it's a Dragon tile. Since the previous player has