nd I need to form a pair. So if I discard J2, maybe I can pair another tile.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard T4, which is part of the Chi, but I have another T4 in the meld. Wait, the Chi is T3, T4, T5. So the T4 in the meld is part of that. The T4 in my hand is a separate one. So if I discard the T4 in my hand, I still have the one in the meld. But that's not helpful for a pair. So maybe keeping the T4 in my hand is better for pairing.\n\nSo which tile is the safest to discard? Maybe a tile that's not part of any potential sequences or pairs. For example, W9. Since W9 is a terminal tile, it's less likely to be needed for a chow. Discarding W9 might be a safe move. Alternatively, T9 is another terminal. B7 is a middle tile. J2 is a dragon.\n\nLooking at the unshown tiles: J2 has 4 left. So if I discard J2, there are three left. T4 has two left. So if I keep T4, I can get another one. But if I discard T4, I can't. So maybe it's better to discard a tile that's not needed for a potential pair or sequence.\n\nLooking at the B2, B3, B4: if I discard one, say B3, then I can still have B2 and B4 left. But without B3, they can't form a chow. So that's bad. So better not to discard any of them.\n\nSimilarly, if I discard B4, then B2 and B3 can't form a chow. So it's better to keep all three.\n\nSo the tiles that are not part of sequences or pairs are W9, T9, B7, J2, T4.\n\nIf I discard W9, T9, B7, or J2, which is the best choice?\n\nJ2 is a dragon. If I discard J2, maybe another player can take it for a meld. But since I have no pungs or kongs of dragons, except maybe J2. If I discard J2, and someone else has two J2s, they can Peng. But if they don't, it's just a discard.\n\nW9 and T9 are terminals. B7 is a middle tile. T4 is a middle tile but part of a sequence.\n\nIf I discard T4, I lose the chance to pair it. So maybe better to discard a terminal. Terminals are less useful for chows, so discarding them might be safer.\n\nBetween W9 and T9, which is better to discard? The u